From 828c9ebc197bd53daa7151be12ba2585bb3762d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 04:56:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(diaspora): merge duplicate Brazil city hubs into one
 Globe marker
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Non-destructive fix for the gap in 0063_diaspora_hub_geography.sql: three
pre-existing Brazil city hubs (Recife, São Luís, São Paulo) fell through the
migration's generic catch-all and ended up with hub_scope='country' but no
country_code and a raw city-name label. Since global-village-pulse.ts
returned one row per hub with no grouping, and DiasporaGlobeFirst.tsx
renders one marker per row, Brazil showed as 4 separate Globe dots instead
of 1 — contradicting the 'one dot per country' redesign goal.

- 0137_diaspora_hub_country_merge.sql: backfills country_code='BR' on the
  3 orphaned rows, adds a self-referencing primary_hub_id column, and points
  them at the canonical Brazil hub (Salvador). No rows deleted, no existing
  hub id / community_id / story / pledge / leader relationship touched.
- New pure function lib/diasporaHubMerge.ts (mergeHubsForGlobeDisplay):
  folds primary_hub_id children into their canonical hub's Globe entry,
  summing member/story/spiral/activity metrics, and exposes the folded-in
  cities as local_hubs for drill-down. Generalizes to any future country
  with multiple legacy city hubs, not Brazil-specific.
- global-village-pulse.ts now returns the merged list; DiasporaGlobeFirst.tsx
  shows a small 'N local hubs' line in the Hub popover when merged.
- New unit tests: diasporaHubMerge.test.ts (4 cases, incl. orphaned-primary
  defensive case).

Verified against a fresh postgis/postgis:16-3.4 database: 145 migrations
apply cleanly, Brazil confirmed as 1 canonical row + 3 primary_hub_id
children, full CI pipeline re-run (typecheck, 49 backend suites/394 tests,
all 10 diaspora contracts, nia-service, App/AI boundary, ESLint
--max-warnings 0, release-validate.js, production-gate) — all green.
